## Decision and scope

Replace subject-only triage in [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) with an evidence-based rule. This is a proposed replacement; the accepted record remains intact. The support desk must adopt it before it changes practice. The [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug and one fix, but its rendered table disagrees with that prose, and its classifier still hides anomalous reports.

The desk supports several products. These findings concern calendar invites in `sched-prod`. They do not establish the same faults in other products. Counts below describe the export through 4 October, not today's live queue.

## Split #4471

<IncidentReview />

The shared parser reads month first even for day-first tenants. Booking-v3 has a different fault: the hint asks for month-first input but the form interprets it using the tenant's day-first order. Fixing the shared parser alone will not fix that hint.

Five reports explicitly noted by the desk describe a one-day move or another timing anomaly. Tenant-based classification assigns them to parser, but their bodies do not establish a day/month reversal. Keep them together for investigation, without claiming they share a proven root cause or asserting a timezone fix. One report says the invite arrived before booking; inspect creation and delivery timestamps as well as timezone conversion. They are excluded from the parser ticket count to avoid double counting.

## Why the queue looked like one incident

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports began in April, while merging into the umbrella ticket began in July. The July merge spike reflects accumulated reports being filed, not necessarily a sudden increase in failures. The desk continued merging afterwards. A count of merged reports is not an incidence rate, and neither a queued fix nor a known label proves resolution.

## Triage rule from now on

1. Read the body and establish the product, intended date, observed date, tenant, tenant locale, booking form and channel.
2. Treat browser locale and intake tags as clues. Check the tenant directory and actual path; the browser may disagree with the tenant.
3. Merge only when evidence supports the same failure mechanism. Matching subjects are insufficient; different subjects can describe the same fault.
4. Keep anomalous or unknown reports separate for investigation. Ask for missing facts rather than merging by default.
5. Deduplicate export rows by report ID. Record a report's destination and preserve its history when splitting tickets.
6. Review incoming reports against these causes, update counts and links, and confirm fixes against each affected path before closing either ticket. Re-read stale guidance before relying on it.

## Compare the current rule with the code

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer shows same-subject reports that have different causes and different-subject reports that share a cause. Its unknown-tenant case does not justify merging. It also shares the classifier's limitation: the body anomalies require reading beyond its result.

## Handoff and verification

The public repository contains one issue for each of the three groups, with report IDs, counts and evidence. For parser, reproduce a day-first date through app/email or booking-v2; preserve the intended day and month in the invite. For hint, reproduce on a day-first booking-v3 form, align the hint with input parsing, and check app/email independently. For timing anomalies, collect the original invite, stored timestamps, event timezone, recipient timezone, booking creation time and delivery time before choosing a fix.

The new rule must be adopted by the desk, and current queue counts must be checked separately. This export does not show whether a queued fix shipped. Update the incident and triage guidance after adoption; leave accepted decision 0001 as history and link its replacement.

